<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dolores Hidalgo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dolores</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dolores Hidalgo is a town in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>. On 16 September 1810 its parish priest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>, rang the church bell and called his parishioners to revolt, the Cry of Dolores that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a>. The date is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s national independence day, and the town, renamed in Hidalgo's honour, is regarded as the cradle of national independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo.jpg" alt="Dolores Hidalgo" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Carlos Fonseca Mata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolores_Hidalgo_Plaza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
